\begin_layout Abstract
Surface Plasmon Polariton in multilayer system is investigated for Ti and
 Si, irradiated in air and in water.
 The effect of the presence of an oxide layer is also considered.
 The results demonstrate that far subwavelength periodicities can be reached
 for several configurations.
 A match with irradiation of Ti and Si in water is found if the presence
 of an oxide layer is taken into account.
 The role of water excitation and oxide layer are presented.
 The resulting periodicity of SPP modes excited in Si immersed into water
 explain the sub-200 nm LIPSS periodicities obtained experimentally.
 The interference of SPPs at different interfaces give birth to a wide range
 of far-subwavelength modes, depending on the layer thickness.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset CommandInset toc
LatexCommand tableofcontents

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
This report focuses on matching the Surface Plasmon Polariton periodicity
 with LIPSS periodicities obtained experimentally in water on Si and on
 Ti.
 Several configurations are tested, and the effect of an oxide layer in
 between is discussed.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In a first part, we propose a summary of the SPP physics at a single interface.
 Then the theory of SPP in a multilayer system is presented.
 In the second part, we briefly sum up the experimental results to explain
 theoretically.
 In a third part, we apply single interface SPP theory and the multilayer
 SPP theory to a common case.
 We thus compare the results and discuss them with literature.
 In a fourth part, we check that free-carrier dynamics in different materials
 allow to reach the densities justifying small SPP periodicities found in
 Section III.
 In a fifth part, we give the analytical proof that Kerr effect is dominant
 and thus show that considering high free-carrier density in both water
 and silicon is reasonable.
 
\end_layout

