\begin_layout Subsection
Verification of the multilayer model
\end_layout

\begin_layout Paragraph
Checking the limit case 
\begin_inset Formula $a\rightarrow0$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In case of two identical interfaces, the SPP wave number is given by Eq.
 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:SimpleSPP"

\end_inset

.
 Performing analytical resolution of dispersion relation (Eq.
 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:ImplicitEq"

\end_inset

) over the multilayer model leads to the analytical solutions, 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\beta_{a=0}\in\left\{ \pm k_{0}\sqrt{\varepsilon_{1}},\pm k_{0}\sqrt{\frac{\varepsilon_{2}\varepsilon_{3}}{\varepsilon_{2}+\varepsilon_{3}}}\right\} 
\]

\end_inset

The first solution describes a propagation mode, and the second describe
 a SPP mode at a single interface made by media II and III.
 Thus, calculating the wave number 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta$
\end_inset

 numerically can lead to the degeneracy that we well identified analytically.
 The degeneracy of the solution can lead to a discontinuous resolution using
 the numerical resolution technique.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Paragraph
Validation on previous works
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
First, the model has been verified by reproducing the results of 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "Prade1991"

\end_inset

.
 The results were reproduced with a very good accordance [see theoretical
 report on HSFL in Ti].
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
We also consider the case of experimental works proposed by 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "Miyaji2012"

\end_inset

.
 Figure 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "fig:Miyaji"

\end_inset

 presents the SPP periodicity as a function of the free-carrier density
 
\begin_inset Formula $N_{e}$
\end_inset

.
 Periodicity is demonstrated for a single interface SPP living at the 
\begin_inset Formula $water/a-Si^{*}$
\end_inset

 interface and at the 
\begin_inset Formula $a-Si^{*}/Si$
\end_inset

 interface.
 It is also calculated at the 
\begin_inset Formula $a-Si^{*}/Si^{*}$
\end_inset

interface.
 The multilayer SPP results are also shown for the case of a large layer
 thickness (
\begin_inset Formula $t=1$
\end_inset

 
\begin_inset Formula $\mu$
\end_inset

m here).
 (i) Comparison with Ref.
 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citep
key "Miyaji2012"

\end_inset

 reveals that invoking 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

standing-wave model
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 allowed to divide the results by a factor 2.
 However, the interference between SPP and laser is included in the dispersion
 relation of SPPs and thus should be avoided.
 (ii) The multilayer SPP results match well with the case of SPP living
 at the 
\begin_inset Formula $water/a-Si^{*}$
\end_inset

 interface.
 This allows us to validate the results obtained with multilayer model and
 to go deeper using different configurations.
 Only the 
\begin_inset Formula $water/a-Si$
\end_inset

 interface leads to SPP excitation since 
\begin_inset Formula $\alpha^{-1}(a-Si)<t$
\end_inset

 where 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

 is the distance between interfaces.
 (iii) None of the previously obtained results well explain the experimental
 periodicity of Si irradiated in water.
 
\end_layout

