\begin_layout Standard
Figure 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "fig:TiO2"

\end_inset

 (a) presents the possible EM wave mode periodicities in the multilayer
 system 
\begin_inset Formula $air-TiO_{2}^{*}-Ti$
\end_inset

.
 It is shown that no special subwavelength mode living in the 
\begin_inset Formula $TiO_{2}^{*}$
\end_inset

 layer.
 This case remains unexplained.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Figure 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "fig:TiO2"

\end_inset

 (b) presents the possible EM mode periodicities in the multilayer system
 
\begin_inset Formula $water^{*}-TiO_{2}-Ti$
\end_inset

.
 It is demonstrated that SPP mode living at the interface 
\begin_inset Formula $water^{*}-TiO_{2}$
\end_inset

 can decrease to 200 nm, and down to 180 nm in the case of extraordinary
 axis of the crystalline orientation of 
\begin_inset Formula $TiO_{2}$
\end_inset

.
 The periodicity of the SPP living in the 
\begin_inset Formula $TiO_{2}$
\end_inset

 layer is distributed around the 
\begin_inset Formula $TiO_{2}/Ti$
\end_inset

 interface, since periodicity of SPP at this interface is 210 nm.
 By increasing density of water, the periodicity of SPP decreases in oxide
 layer, especially if the thickness of the oxide layer decreases to several
 nanometers.
 In that case, sub-100 nm periodicities become possible to reach.
 The experimental measurement reported in Table 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "tab:Ripples-experimental-periodicity"

\end_inset

 has been added to the Figure 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "fig:TiO2"

\end_inset

 (b).
 These results demonstrate that considering the excitation of the surrounding
 medium e.g.
 water allows to explain the sub-200 nm periodicity in case of a presence
 of an oxide layer at the surface of Ti.
 A very good match is found for a oxide layer thickness of 20 nm, which
 is reasonable after several irradiations.
 Moreover, the required densities in water can be reached according to 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "Noack1999"

\end_inset

, and our calculations in Section 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "sec:Free-carrier-dynamics"

\end_inset

.
 
\end_layout

