 (b) presents the periodicity of SPPs calculated for the configuration 
\begin_inset Formula $Water^{*}/SiO_{2}/Si$
\end_inset

.
 The periodicity at the 
\begin_inset Formula $water^{*}-SiO_{2}$
\end_inset

 interface (calculated using Eq 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:SimpleSPP"

\end_inset

) is shown in blue and is equal to the periodicity in presence of an oxide
 layer of 
\begin_inset Formula $2$
\end_inset

 
\begin_inset Formula $\mu m$
\end_inset

 (calculated by solving Eq.
 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:ImplicitEq"

\end_inset

).
 This result validates the multilayer model and allows to consider smaller
 oxide thicknesses.
 Note that the periodicity at high density tends to 
\begin_inset Formula $\lambda/\Re e\sqrt{\varepsilon_{SiO_{2}}}$
\end_inset

, and that SPP are not defined for 
\begin_inset Formula $n_{e}<n_{cr}$
\end_inset

.
 The curves involving 3 interfaces demonstrate that periodicity of SPPs
 in presence of a thin oxide layer and excited water have a periodicity
 
\begin_inset Formula $\Lambda<100$
\end_inset

 nm.
 A good agreement is found with 
\begin_inset Formula $t=10$
\end_inset

 nm for reachable densities 
\begin_inset Formula $N_{e}=8\times10^{27}\, m^{-3}$
\end_inset

 in modification fluence regime.
 The periodicity of SPP in multilayer as a function of free-carrier density
 reached in water shows the same behavior than SPP at the 
\begin_inset Formula $water^{*}/SiO_{2}$
\end_inset

 layer, but with a lower periodicity.
 This suggests that presence of a thin oxide film over the Si surface is
 important and plays a role in the reduction of the periodicity.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
As a conclusion of our investigations, we obtained two configurations matching
 with experimental results (on Si and Ti irradiated in water) by considering
 two key points: (i) excitation of the surrounding medium (water in this
 case), (ii) presence of an oxide layer, which has been demonstrated as
 being important by chemical analysis.
 The multilayer system with excited water and oxide thus well explains the
 periodicity reduction in liquids.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
\begin_inset Formula $water^{*}/SiO_{2}/Si^{*}$
\end_inset

 system
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Since ripples are observed at the surface of Si inside water, it turns out
 that Si is excited.
 Following results of Ref.
 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citep
key "Bonse2009"

\end_inset

, we assume that modification density threshold of Si is equal in water
 and is air.
 We thus set the free-carrier density of Si to 
\begin_inset Formula $4.6\times10^{21}$
\end_inset

 
\begin_inset Formula $cm^{-3}$
\end_inset

 in the experimental conditions of the current work (water medium, 
\begin_inset Formula $F=0.12$
\end_inset

 
\begin_inset Formula $J/cm^{2}$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $\tau=30$
\end_inset

 fs).
 
