\begin_layout Plain Layout
\begin_inset Caption

\begin_layout Plain Layout
\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "fig:SPPWaterStarSiO2SiStar"

\end_inset

Periodicity of SPPs at the interface 
\begin_inset Formula $Water^{*}-SiO_{2}-Si^{*}.$
\end_inset

 Density in Si has been fixed at the damage threshold 
\begin_inset Formula $n_{e}=4.6\times10^{21}$
\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula $cm^{-3}$
\end_inset

.
 (a) Effective mass is equal to 
\begin_inset Formula $1$
\end_inset

 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citep
key "Sarpe2012"

\end_inset

; (b) Effective mass is equal to 
\begin_inset Formula $0.5$
\end_inset

.
 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citep
key "Noack1999"

\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\end_inset

Figure 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "fig:SPPWaterStarSiO2SiStar"

\end_inset

 shows the possible periodicity in the 
\begin_inset Formula $Water^{*}/SiO_{2}/Si^{*}$
\end_inset

 configuration.
 Below critical density of water (
\begin_inset Formula $n_{cr}^{H_{2}O}=3.26\times10^{21}$
\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula $cm^{-3}$
\end_inset

), periodicity follows 
\begin_inset Formula $\Lambda=\lambda/n_{H_{2}O}^{*}$
\end_inset

 the propagation mode of EM waves in water.
 Above this density, SPP mode is excited and leads to a substantial decrease
 of the periodicity down to 50 nm in case of a ultra-thin (5 nm) oxide layer.
 With a 10 nm oxide layer, the periodicity corresponds to the experimental
 observations.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
A resonance cutting is observed at high density in water.
 Indeed, a discontinuity is observed if 
\begin_inset Formula $n_{e}>2\times10^{22}$
\end_inset

 
\begin_inset Formula $cm^{-2}$
\end_inset

.
 The periodicity at high density in water converges smoothly to the propagation
 mode of EM waves in silicon [
\begin_inset Formula $k^{2}=\frac{\omega^{2}}{c^{2}}\varepsilon_{Si}\Leftrightarrow\Lambda=\lambda/\Re e\left(\varepsilon_{Si}\right)$
\end_inset

] at the fixed density.
 In the case of a low density of free-carriers present in Si CB, this behavior
 was also observed by an absence of possible wavenumbers.
 Thus, we conclude that this mode is sensitive to the excitation of Si.
 This effect correspond to a coupled mode involving the three media at the
 same time i.e.
 to coupled SPP modes.
 It would be of interest to test the effect of the Si density in a more
 detailed way.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
We could extend these results by working on the imaginary part of the SPP
 wavenumber 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta$
\end_inset

.
 For example, this could explain the transition between propagation and
 SPP regime by following the criterion 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\alpha_{SPP}=2\Im m\left(\beta\right)>\alpha_{interband}=2\Im m\left(k\right)=2\frac{\omega}{c}\sqrt{\varepsilon_{Si}^{*}}
\]

\end_inset

This would lead to a upper limit of density to excite the Surface Plasmon
 Polariton, since above this limit, the SPPs do not couple with laser anymore.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
As a consequence, the thin film of silicon dioxide formed at the surface
 of silicon has lead to a strong decrease of the periodicity while water
 electrons are excited, in presence of an oxide layer covering the Si sample.
 This result demonstrates that it exists a density for which the SPP living
 in the thin dielectric layer has a periodicity of 100 nm while water is
 excited.
 Excitation level of Si only acts on the upper limit of the density to couple
 with SPP modes.
 The high densities considered for the SPP mode will be demonstrated to
 be reached in the Section ##.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
However, it is not justified that free-carrier density required to damage
 Si (
\begin_inset Formula $n_{e}^{Si}=4.6\times10^{21}$
\end_inset

cm
\begin_inset Formula $^{-3}$
\end_inset

) is lower than for the optical breakdown of water.
 We have found that, by considering the optical mass of water 
\begin_inset Formula $m_{H_{2}O}^{*}=0.5$
\end_inset

 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citep
key "Noack1999"

\end_inset

, the period follows a similar behavior, but for lower densities.
 Using this optical mass allows that it exists 
\begin_inset Formula $n_{e}^{(H_{2}O)}$
\end_inset

 for which the periodicity corresponds to the experimental one, and for
 which 
\begin_inset Formula $n_{e}^{(H_{2}O)}<n_{e}^{(Si)}$
\end_inset

.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Free-carrier dynamics in 
\begin_inset Formula $Si$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $H_{2}O$
\end_inset


