\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Figure 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "fig:Maximum-free-carrier-density"

\end_inset

 also presents the maximum free-carrier density obtained in water.
 Results show that the density of free-carriers in water is lower than free-carr
ier density inside Si at a given laser fluence.
 However, (i) the thickness of the water layer in front on the Si sample
 is about 5 mm, (ii) and Kerr effect in water is stronger than in Si.
 These conditions are sufficient to induce self-focusing effects, as filamentati
on is observed experimentally.
 We now demonstrate that these effects are important.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The required free-carrier density for ablation of the Si material is of
 the order of 
\begin_inset Formula $5\times10^{21}$
\end_inset

cm
\begin_inset Formula $^{-3}$
\end_inset

, and the laser fluence which allows ablation is consistent with the experimenta
lly identified ablation threshold for a pulse duration of 
\begin_inset Formula $\tau=30$
\end_inset

 fs.
 Since ripples are observed at the sample surface, this requires that sample
 has been exposed to a rather high energy, corresponding excitation level
 occurring during ablation.
 Such fluence is however not sufficient to induce a so high density of free-carr
iers in water.
 Then, the excitation of the water will always been reduced in front of
 the one of silicon.
 After revision, the periodicities provided by considering the optical mass
 proposed by 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "Noack1999"

\end_inset

 
\begin_inset Formula $m'=0.5$
\end_inset

 can be reached for densities of 
\begin_inset Formula $\sim2.5\times10^{21}$
\end_inset

 cm
\begin_inset Formula $^{-3}$
\end_inset

.
 However, following the calculations we performed in this section, such
 density of water electrons is still not reached at the considered laser
 fluences.
 Even if we consider that water actually reaches this level of excitation,
 it means something has to decrease the energy reaching the Si sample.
 It is possible that the thin film of 
\begin_inset Formula $SiO_{2}$
\end_inset

 is absorbing a part of the energy preventing the Si is too much excited.
 But then it requires that we also consider the excitation of the 
\begin_inset Formula $SiO_{2}$
\end_inset

 layer.
 Let's try with several values of excitation of the 
\begin_inset Formula $SiO_{2}$
\end_inset

 (
\begin_inset Formula $n_{e}(SiO_{2})\in[10^{25},10^{26},10^{27}]$
\end_inset

 
\begin_inset Formula $m^{-3}$
\end_inset

.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Kerr effect in water
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In experimental conditions using a water vessel, it is difficult to know
 precisely the physical parameters such as distance, laser energy, and spot
 size on the sample.
 Indeed, sensors cannot be used inside water, thus spot size, laser intensity
 and exact position of the sample are not well-known.
 We will then consider a theoretical approach in order to be able to calculate
 the range of intensities reaching the Si sample.
 We will conclude that, as observed on the SEM pictures, Kerr effect plays
 a major role and leads to the excitation of both water and Si.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The experimental parameters which are known with a good precision are: laser
 wavelength 
\begin_inset Formula $790$
\end_inset

 nm, pulse duration 
\begin_inset Formula $30$
\end_inset

 fs, focal length of the spherical mirror 
\begin_inset Formula $f=500$
\end_inset

 mm, diameter of the laser beam before the length is 
\begin_inset Formula $\textrm{Ø}=10=2w_{01}$
\end_inset

 mm, waist of the laser beam is estimated at 
\begin_inset Formula $z_{1}=1.5$
\end_inset

 m, spot size before the water vessel is 
\begin_inset Formula $w_{1}=140$
\end_inset

 
\begin_inset Formula $\mu m$
\end_inset

, and laser fluence was also measured at the position 
\begin_inset Formula $z_{M}=-10$
\end_inset

 mm.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Following relation linking waist spot sizes of a lens with incident gaussian
 beam parameters 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citep
key "Weichel1976"

\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula 
\[
w_{0}^{-2}=\left[w_{01}^{-2}\left(1-\frac{z_{1}}{f}\right)^{2}+f^{-2}\left(\frac{\pi w_{01}}{\lambda}\right)^{2}\right]^{-1},
\]

\end_inset

with 
\begin_inset Formula $z_{1}=1.5$
\end_inset

 m is the distance between lens and beam waist place before the spherical
 mirror (focal length 
\begin_inset Formula $f=500$
\end_inset

 mm) on the optical path, we find that beam waist after spherical mirror
 is 
\begin_inset Formula $w_{0}=25.15$
\end_inset

 
\begin_inset Formula $\mu m$
\end_inset

.
 The sample has been positioned nearly 
\begin_inset Formula $\left|z_{M}\right|=10$
\end_inset

 mm before the beam waist in water.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The importance of the Kerr effect can be demonstrated by calculating the
 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

B integral
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citep
key "Momma1998"

\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
B=\frac{2\pi}{\lambda}\int_{z=0}^{z=L}I(z)n_{2}dz\label{eq:Bintegral}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

which, if 
\begin_inset Formula $B>2\pi$
\end_inset

, demonstrates the importance of the non-linear refraction effects (
\begin_inset Formula $n_{2}=2.8\times10^{-20}$
\end_inset

 
\begin_inset Formula $m^{2}/W$
\end_inset

 is the Kerr effect coefficient in water 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citep
key "Weber2003"

\end_inset

).
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Gaussian beam distribution of intensity is given by 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
I(t,r,z)=I_{0}e^{-2\left(\frac{r}{w(z)}\right)^{2}}\left(\frac{w_{0}}{w(z)}\right)^{2}e^{-\frac{1}{2}\left(\frac{t}{\sigma_{\tau}}\right)^{2}}
\]

\end_inset

with 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
w(z)=w_{0}\sqrt{1+\left(\frac{z}{z_{R}}\right)^{2}}
\]

\end_inset

 
\begin_inset Formula $z_{R}=\frac{\pi w_{0}^{2}}{\lambda}$
\end_inset

 is the Rayleigh length equal to 
\begin_inset Formula $2.51$
\end_inset

 mm i.e.
 lower than the distance between waist and sample.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Intensity has been measured experimentally at nearly 
\begin_inset Formula $10$
\end_inset

 mm from the beam waist.
 The definition of fluence for a Gaussian beam is given by 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
F(z)=\int_{t=-4\tau}^{t=4\tau}\int_{r=0}^{r\rightarrow\infty}2\pi r\, I(t,r,z)\, dr\, dt
\]

\end_inset

Experimentally, the fluence measured at 
\begin_inset Formula $z_{M}=-10$
\end_inset

 mm from the beam waist is given by 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
F_{exp}(z_{M})=\frac{2E}{\pi w_{1}^{2}}
\]

\end_inset

As a first approximation, we neglect the effect of water optical index to
 express the intensity distribution.
 We find 
\begin_inset Formula $I_{0}$
\end_inset

 by solving the energy 
\begin_inset Formula $E$
\end_inset

 conservation along the beam axis 
\begin_inset Formula $z$
\end_inset

 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
E(z_{M})=\frac{\pi w_{1}^{2}}{2}\int_{t=-4\tau}^{t=4\tau}\int_{r=0}^{r\rightarrow\infty}2\pi r\, I(t,r,z)\, dr\, dt=\frac{F_{exp}}{2}\pi w_{1}^{2}
\]

\end_inset

As a result, we find that at fluence 
\begin_inset Formula $0.1$
\end_inset

 
\begin_inset Formula $J/cm^{2}$
\end_inset

 and pulse duration 
\begin_inset Formula $\tau=30$
\end_inset

 fs, intensity at the beam waist is 
\begin_inset Formula $I_{0}=9.7071\times10^{13}$
\end_inset

 
\begin_inset Formula $W/cm^{2}$
\end_inset

, and the peak intensity at the sample position is 
\begin_inset Formula $I(0,0,z_{M})=5.772\times10^{12}$
\end_inset

 
\begin_inset Formula $W/cm^{2}$
\end_inset

.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
We can now calculate the value of B-integral given in Eq.
 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand eqref
reference "eq:Bintegral"

\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula 
\[
B\cong600.53\gg2\pi.
\]

\end_inset

This result demonstrates that non-linear Kerr effect is dominating the propagati
on in water medium on the length of 
\begin_inset Formula $L=5$
\end_inset

 mm.
 We also found that intensity at the target of Si sample is greater than
 the breakdown threshold at short pulse duration proposed by 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citep
key "Feng1997"

\end_inset

.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Conclusions
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
A multilayer SPP model has been studied and applied to different configurations
 involving Ti, Si in air and water, with and without of an oxide layer of
 thickness 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

.
 In each case, it has been demonstrated that a layer thickness leads to
 reproduce the SPP periodicities involving one simple interface.
 The allowed to validate the model to use it for deeper investigations.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The dispersion relation has been calculated numerically and describes both
 propagative modes (
\begin_inset Formula $\propto\lambda/n^{*}$
\end_inset

) and SPP modes, and is also valid to describe the coupled interface SPP
 modes.
 The condition to excite SPP modes 
\begin_inset Formula $\Re e\left(\varepsilon\right)=0$
\end_inset

 also applies in the case of a multilayer system and thus, the required
 threshold density 
\begin_inset Formula $n_{e}$
\end_inset

 corresponds to the so-called 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

critical density
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 
\begin_inset Formula $n_{cr}$
\end_inset

 or 
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

screening density
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 in solids.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Sub-200 nm periodicities of LIPSS formed at the surface of Si and Ti in
 water environment are explained by the excitation of Surface Plasmon Polariton
 at the 
\begin_inset Formula $water^{*}/oxide$
\end_inset

 interface, requiring excitation of the water by the laser.
 It has been demonstrated that the resulting periodicities explain for the
 first time the observed 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

tiny
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 LSFL structures at the interface of Ti and Si irradiated in water.
 The role of the oxide layer and the excitation of the surrounding medium
 are responsible for the reduction of the SPP periodicity, by interference
 between SPP modes at the interfaces of the oxide layer.
 
