\begin_layout Plain Layout
Appendix: Gaussian optics
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout
For a scalar electric field 
\begin_inset Formula $E\in\mathbb{C}$
\end_inset

 (polarization effects are neglected),we can write Helmoltz equation 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\left(\nabla^{2}+k^{2}\right)E=0
\]

\end_inset

We express electric field by 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
E(x,y,z)=u(x,y,z)e^{-ikz}
\]

\end_inset

which leads to solve the following Eq.
 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\nabla^{2}u-2ik\frac{\partial u}{\partial z}=0
\]

\end_inset

Paraxial approximation consists in writing 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\frac{\partial^{2}u}{\partial z^{2}}\ll2k\frac{\partial u}{\partial z}
\]

\end_inset

 and thus to neglect the term 
\begin_inset Formula $\nabla_{z}^{2}u$
\end_inset

 in front of the others, leading to write 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\nabla_{x,y}^{2}u-2ik\frac{\partial u}{\partial z}=0
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The free-carrier densities required to justify these results have been calculate
d as a function of laser fluence in water, and in Si immersed in air or
 water.
 Results showed that such densities are reached in Si in air and water medium
 at 
\begin_inset Formula $30$
\end_inset

 fs, 
\begin_inset Formula $0.1$
\end_inset

 J/cm
\begin_inset Formula $^{2}$
\end_inset

.
 However, the pulse propagation in water is very sensitive to Kerr effect,
 which prevents any control over the intensity reaching the sample.
 Indeed, calculation of the B-integral demonstrated that Kerr effect is
 very important during the propagation.
 This effect justifies how free-carrier density can reach very high values
 in water, sufficiently to justify the far subwavelength periodicities,
 matching with experimental measurements.
 
